**8. Code Review Checklist for Tests**

✅ Tests are independent and can run in any order
✅ No hardcoded values (use constants/config)
✅ Page Objects used appropriately
✅ Waits are explicit, not Thread.sleep()
✅ Assertions have clear failure messages
✅ Test names describe scenario
✅ No test logic in page objects
✅ Configuration externalized
✅ Error handling with screenshots
✅ Documentation for complex scenarios

**The Most Important Pattern in Test Automation:**

Page Object Model (POM) is the foundation of maintainable test automation. It creates an abstraction layer between your tests and the UI.

**Why Page Object Model?**

```
Without POM:
@Test
public void testLogin() {
    driver.findElement(By.id("username")).sendKeys("testuser");
    driver.findElement(By.id("password")).sendKeys("pass123");
    driver.findElement(By.id("login")).click();
    assertTrue(driver.findElement(By.id("welcome")).isDisplayed());
}

Problems:
- UI change breaks all tests
- Duplicate locators everywhere
- Tests hard to read
- Maintenance nightmare

With POM:
LoginPage loginPage = new LoginPage(driver);
loginPage.login("testuser", "pass123");
assertTrue(loginPage.isWelcomeMessageDisplayed());

Benefits:
- UI changes: update one page object
- Clean, readable tests
- Reusable components
```

**1. Basic Page Object Structure**

```java
public class LoginPage {
    private AppiumDriver driver;
    private WebDriverWait wait;

    // Locators - store centrally
    private By usernameField = By.id("username");
    private By passwordField = By.id("password");
    private By loginButton = By.id("login");
    private By welcomeMessage = By.id("welcome");

    public LoginPage(AppiumDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        this.wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, 10);
    }

    // Actions - what you can do on this page
    public void enterUsername(String username) {
        wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(usernameField))
            .sendKeys(username);
    }

    public void enterPassword(String password) {
        driver.findElement(passwordField).sendKeys(password);
    }

    public void clickLogin() {
        driver.findElement(loginButton).click();
    }

    public void login(String username, String password) {
        enterUsername(username);
        enterPassword(password);
        clickLogin();
    }

    // Queries - get information from page
    public String getWelcomeMessage() {
        return wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(welcomeMessage))
            .getText();
    }

    public boolean isWelcomeMessageDisplayed() {
        try {
            return driver.findElement(welcomeMessage).isDisplayed();
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

**2. Cross-Platform Page Objects**

```java
public class LoginPage {
    private AppiumDriver driver;
    private WebDriverWait wait;
    private boolean isAndroid;

    // Platform-specific locators
    private By usernameField;
    private By passwordField;
    private By loginButton;
    private By welcomeMessage;

    public LoginPage(AppiumDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        this.wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, 10);
        this.isAndroid = driver instanceof AndroidDriver;

        initLocators();
    }

    private void initLocators() {
        if (isAndroid) {
            usernameField = By.id("com.example.app:id/username");
            passwordField = By.id("com.example.app:id/password");
            loginButton = By.id("com.example.app:id/login");
            welcomeMessage = By.id("com.example.app:id/welcome");
        } else {
            usernameField = By.xpath("//XCUIElementTypeTextField[@name='username']");
            passwordField = By.xpath("//XCUIElementTypeSecureTextField[@name='password']");
            loginButton = By.xpath("//XCUIElementTypeButton[@name='login']");
            welcomeMessage = By.xpath("//XCUIElementTypeStaticText[@name='welcome']");
        }
    }

    // Rest of methods same for both platforms
}
```

**3. Using Appium Page Factory**

```java
public class LoginPage {
    private AppiumDriver driver;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/username")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "username")
    private MobileElement usernameField;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/password")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "password")
    private MobileElement passwordField;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/login")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "login")
    private MobileElement loginButton;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/welcome")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "welcome")
    private MobileElement welcomeMessage;

    public LoginPage(AppiumDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        PageFactory.initElements(new AppiumFieldDecorator(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10)), this);
    }

    public void login(String username, String password) {
        usernameField.sendKeys(username);
        passwordField.sendKeys(password);
        loginButton.click();
    }

    public String getWelcomeMessage() {
        return welcomeMessage.getText();
    }
}
```

**4. Page Object Best Practices**

✅ **One Page Object per screen**
✅ **Methods return other Page Objects**

```java
public class LoginPage {
    public HomePage login(String username, String password) {
        // login logic
        return new HomePage(driver);
    }
}

// Usage:
HomePage home = loginPage.login("user", "pass");
```

✅ **Don't expose WebElements**

```java
// BAD
public WebElement getUsernameField() {
    return usernameField;
}

// GOOD
public void enterUsername(String username) {
    usernameField.sendKeys(username);
}
```

✅ **Don't put assertions in Page Objects**

```java
// BAD
public void assertWelcomeMessageDisplayed() {
    assertTrue(welcomeMessage.isDisplayed());
}

// GOOD
public boolean isWelcomeMessageDisplayed() {
    return welcomeMessage.isDisplayed();
}

// In test:
assertTrue(loginPage.isWelcomeMessageDisplayed());
```

✅ **Handle waits in Page Objects**

```java
public String getWelcomeMessage() {
    wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOf(welcomeMessage));
    return welcomeMessage.getText();
}
```

**5. Component Object Pattern**

For reusable UI components:

```java
public class NavigationBar {
    private AppiumDriver driver;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/home")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "home")
    private MobileElement homeButton;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/search")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "search")
    private MobileElement searchButton;

    @AndroidFindBy(id = "com.example.app:id/profile")
    @iOSXCUITFindBy(accessibility = "profile")
    private MobileElement profileButton;

    public NavigationBar(AppiumDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        PageFactory.initElements(new AppiumFieldDecorator(driver), this);
    }

    public HomePage goToHome() {
        homeButton.click();
        return new HomePage(driver);
    }

    public SearchPage goToSearch() {
        searchButton.click();
        return new SearchPage(driver);
    }

    public ProfilePage goToProfile() {
        profileButton.click();
        return new ProfilePage(driver);
    }
}

// Used in any page that has navigation bar
public class HomePage {
    private NavigationBar navBar;

    public HomePage(AppiumDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        this.navBar = new NavigationBar(driver);
    }

    public SearchPage search() {
        return navBar.goToSearch();
    }
}
```

**6. Flow Objects**

For multi-page workflows:

```java
public class RegistrationFlow {
    private AppiumDriver driver;
    private WelcomePage welcomePage;
    private SignupPage signupPage;
    private VerificationPage verificationPage;

    public RegistrationFlow(AppiumDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
        this.welcomePage = new WelcomePage(driver);
        this.signupPage = new SignupPage(driver);
        this.verificationPage = new VerificationPage(driver);
