**Handling Timing Issues in Web Applications:**

**1. Why Synchronization Matters**

```
Without synchronization:
Test → Find element → Element not loaded yet → Test fails ❌

With synchronization:
Test → Wait for element → Element loads → Interaction works ✓
```

**2. Implicit Wait**

```java
// Set once, applies to all element searches
driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

// How it works:
// If element not found immediately, WebDriver polls DOM for up to 10 seconds

// Best for: Simple scripts, consistent apps
// Drawback: Can slow down tests when elements are truly missing
```

**3. Explicit Wait**

```java
// Wait for specific condition
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

WebElement element = wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id("dynamic"))
);

// Common Expected Conditions:
ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(locator)
ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator)
ExpectedConditions.presenceOfElementLocated(locator)
ExpectedConditions.invisibilityOfElementLocated(locator)
ExpectedConditions.textToBePresentInElement(locator, "text")
ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
ExpectedConditions.titleContains("title")
ExpectedConditions.urlContains("path")
```

**4. Fluent Wait**

```java
// More configurable wait
Wait<WebDriver> fluent = new FluentWait<WebDriver>(driver)
    .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .pollingEvery(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class)
    .ignoring(StaleElementReferenceException.class);

WebElement element = fluent.until(
    driver -> driver.findElement(By.id("dynamic"))
);
```

**5. Custom Wait Conditions**

```java
// Custom expected condition
public static ExpectedCondition<Boolean> elementSizeAtLeast(int minSize) {
    return new ExpectedCondition<Boolean>() {
        @Override
        public Boolean apply(WebDriver driver) {
            List<WebElement> elements = driver.findElements(By.className("item"));
            return elements.size() >= minSize;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return "element size to be at least " + minSize;
        }
    };
}

// Usage
wait.until(elementSizeAtLeast(5));
```

**6. Wait with Function**

```java
// Using Function interface
WebElement element = wait.until(new Function<WebDriver, WebElement>() {
    public WebElement apply(WebDriver driver) {
        WebElement el = driver.findElement(By.id("dynamic"));
        return el.isDisplayed() ? el : null;
    }
});

// Java 8 lambda
WebElement element = wait.until(driver -> {
    WebElement el = driver.findElement(By.id("dynamic"));
    return el.isDisplayed() ? el : null;
});
```

**7. Sleep (Avoid if possible)**

```java
// NOT recommended - use waits instead
Thread.sleep(3000); // Always waits 3 seconds, even if element loads earlier
```

**8. Page Load Wait**

```java
// Wait for page to load
driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

// Wait for AJAX to complete (with jQuery)
wait.until(driver -> (Boolean) ((JavascriptExecutor) driver)
    .executeScript("return jQuery.active == 0"));

// Wait for document ready state
wait.until(driver -> ((JavascriptExecutor) driver)
    .executeScript("return document.readyState").equals("complete"));
```

**9. Wait Strategies Comparison**

| Strategy | Scope | Flexibility | Performance | Use Case |
|----------|-------|-------------|-------------|----------|
| **Implicit Wait** | Global | Low | Moderate | Simple apps |
| **Explicit Wait** | Element-specific | High | Good | Most scenarios |
| **Fluent Wait** | Element-specific | Very High | Good | Complex conditions |
| **Thread.sleep** | Fixed | None | Poor | Almost never |

**10. Best Practices**

✅ Use explicit waits for specific elements
✅ Set implicit wait as a backup (10 seconds)
✅ Avoid `Thread.sleep()`
✅ Use ExpectedConditions when possible
✅ Handle `StaleElementReferenceException` with retries
✅ Wait for element state, not just presence
✅ Use reasonable timeout values
✅ Log wait failures for debugging

**11. Complete Synchronization Example**

```java
public class WaitExample {
    private WebDriver driver;
    private WebDriverWait wait;

    public WaitExample() {
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
        // Set implicit wait as backup
        driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

        // Set explicit wait
        wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
    }

    public void testDynamicElement() {
        driver.get("https://example.com/dynamic");

        // Wait for element to be clickable
        WebElement button = wait.until(
            ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("loadButton"))
        );
        button.click();

        // Wait for new element
        wait.until(
            ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id("loadedContent"))
        );

        // Wait for text
        wait.until(
            ExpectedConditions.textToBePresentInElementLocated(
                By.id("status"), "Complete"
            )
        );

        // Wait for invisibility of loading spinner
        wait.until(
            ExpectedConditions.invisibilityOfElementLocated(By.className("spinner"))
        );
    }
}
```

**12. Handling Common Synchronization Issues**

| Issue | Solution |
|-------|----------|
| **Element not found** | Use explicit wait with visibility condition |
| **Stale element** | Relocate element before interacting |
| **Element not clickable** | Wait for elementToBeClickable |
| **Page not loaded** | Use pageLoadTimeout or wait for element |
| **AJAX content** | Wait for AJAX to complete or element to appear |
| **Animations** | Wait for element to be stable |
| **Redirects** | Wait for URL to change |

