**Powerful XML Path Language for Complex Selections:**

XPath (XML Path Language) is a query language for selecting nodes from XML documents. In HTML pages, it's incredibly powerful for finding elements when simpler locators won't work.

**1. Absolute XPath**

Starts from the root (`/html`) and goes down through every level.

```xpath
/html/body/div[3]/div[2]/form/div[4]/input
/html/body/div[2]/div[1]/div[2]/a
```

```java
// Java - Absolute XPath (brittle, avoid)
driver.findElement(By.xpath("/html/body/div[3]/div[2]/form/div[4]/input"));
```

**Problems with Absolute XPath:**

❌ **Brittle** – Breaks with any structural change
❌ **Hard to read** – Long, cryptic paths
❌ **Slow** – Traverses entire DOM
❌ **Not maintainable** – Any layout change breaks tests

**2. Relative XPath**

Starts from anywhere (`//`) and searches for elements based on attributes, text, or position.

```xpath
// Basic relative XPath
//input[@id='username']
//button[@class='login']
//div[@name='address']
```

```java
// Java - Relative XPath (preferred)
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//input[@id='username']"));
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//button[text()='Login']"));
```

**3. XPath Axes**

Navigate through the DOM tree in different directions.

```xpath
// Following axis - elements after current
//div[@id='main']/following::p

// Preceding axis - elements before current
//div[@id='footer']/preceding::div

// Parent axis
//input[@id='username']/parent::div

// Child axis (default)
//div[@id='form']/child::input

// Ancestor axis
//input[@id='username']/ancestor::form

// Descendant axis (same as //)
//form/descendant::input

// Following-sibling - elements at same level after
//h2[@id='title']/following-sibling::p

// Preceding-sibling - elements at same level before
//h2[@id='title']/preceding-sibling::h1
```

**4. XPath Functions**

```xpath
// Text functions
//button[text()='Submit']
//a[contains(text(), 'Click')]
//div[starts-with(text(), 'Welcome')]

// Attribute functions
//input[starts-with(@id, 'user_')]
//div[contains(@class, 'error')]

// Position functions
//li[1]                    // First li
//li[last()]               // Last li
//li[position() < 3]       // First two li's
//tr[position() mod 2 = 1] // Odd rows

// Logical operators
//input[@type='text' and @name='username']
//button[@class='btn' or @class='button']
//div[not(@class='hidden')]
```

**5. XPath with Multiple Attributes**

```xpath
// And condition
//input[@type='text' and @name='username']
//div[@class='product' and @data-id='123']

// Or condition
//button[@class='btn-primary' or @class='btn-secondary']

// Mixed conditions
//input[(@type='text' or @type='email') and @required='required']
```

**6. XPath by Text Content**

```xpath
// Exact text match
//button[text()='Submit Form']

// Contains text
//div[contains(text(), 'Error message')]

// Text with normalize-space (handles extra whitespace)
//div[normalize-space()='Welcome to our site']

// Multiple text conditions
//p[contains(text(), 'Success') and contains(text(), 'completed')]
```

**7. XPath Indexing**

```xpath
// First element
(//div[@class='item'])[1]

// Last element
(//div[@class='item'])[last()]

// Specific index
(//tr)[3]/td[2]

// With conditions
//ul[@class='menu']/li[3]
```

**8. Dynamic XPath Examples**

```java
// Find input based on label text
By.xpath("//label[text()='Username']/following-sibling::input");

// Find row containing specific text, then get its checkbox
By.xpath("//tr[contains(td, 'John Doe')]/td/input[@type='checkbox']");

// Find element by data attribute
By.xpath("//div[@data-testid='product-card']");

// Find element by partial attribute match
By.xpath("//a[contains(@href, '/product/')]");

// Find element by multiple possible class values
By.xpath("//div[contains(concat(' ', @class, ' '), ' error ') or contains(concat(' ', @class, ' '), ' alert ')]");
```

**9. XPath Best Practices**

✅ **Use relative XPath** – Never use absolute paths
✅ **Prefer IDs and attributes** – Over index-based selection
✅ **Use contains() for dynamic values** – When IDs change
✅ **Keep XPath readable** – Break long XPaths into variables
✅ **Test in browser console** – Use `$x("//div")` to verify
✅ **Avoid indexing when possible** – Unless order is stable

**10. XPath Performance Tips**

- **`//` is expensive** – It searches entire DOM
- **Use specific paths** – `/html/body/div` is faster than `//div`
- **Prefer attributes over text** – Attribute lookup is faster
- **Avoid complex functions** – Simple paths execute faster
- **Use descendant axis sparingly** – `/` is faster than `//`

**11. Common XPath Patterns**

| Scenario | XPath |
|----------|-------|
| **Input by label** | `//label[text()='Email']/following-sibling::input` |
| **Checkbox in row** | `//tr[contains(td, 'Product Name')]//input[@type='checkbox']` |
| **Button by partial class** | `//button[contains(@class, 'submit')]` |
| **Element with data attribute** | `//div[@data-role='modal']` |
| **Nth element of type** | `(//div[@class='item'])[3]` |
| **Element after another** | `//h2[text()='Details']/following-sibling::div` |
| **Element before another** | `//div[@id='footer']/preceding-sibling::div` |
| **Parent of element** | `//input[@id='username']/..` |
| **Elements with two classes** | `//div[contains(@class, 'card') and contains(@class, 'featured')]` |

**12. XPath vs CSS Selectors**

| Feature | XPath | CSS Selectors |
|---------|-------|---------------|
| **Speed** | Slower | Faster |
| **Text search** | Yes (`text()`) | No |
| **Parent traversal** | Yes (`/..`) | No |
| **Sibling traversal** | Yes (`following-sibling`) | Yes (`+`, `~`) |
| **Complex conditions** | Yes (and/or) | Limited |
| **Readability** | Can be verbose | Clean |
| **Browser support** | All browsers | All browsers |
| **Learning curve** | Steeper | Easier |

