**The Art of Efficient Test Scripts:**

Well-optimized scripts generate more load with fewer resources, run longer without degradation, and produce more reliable results.

**1. Variable Scope and Usage**

```
// User Defined Variables (at test plan level)
// Available to all threads, evaluated once at start
[ User Defined Variables ]
Name: API_VERSION  Value: v2
Name: DEFAULT_USER_AGENT Value: JMeter-Test

// Properties (set via command line)
// Can be changed during test
${__P(users,100)}  // Get property with default

// Thread-local variables (set during test)
// Created with extractors or JSR223
${userId}  // Available only in current thread

// Best practices:
// - Use UDV for constants
// - Use properties for configurable values
// - Use thread-local variables for dynamic data
// - Avoid ${__eval()} and ${__evalVar()} when possible
```

**2. Efficient Correlation Patterns**

```
// Pattern 1: Single extraction
[ HTTP Request: Login ]
    [ JSON Extractor ]
    Variable: authToken
    JSON Path: $.data.token

// Pattern 2: Multiple extractions (use single extractor)
[ HTTP Request: Get Product ]
    [ JSON Extractor ]  ← One extractor, multiple paths
    Names: productId, productName, productPrice
    JSON Paths: $.id, $.name, $.price

// Pattern 3: Dynamic number of values
[ HTTP Request: Search ]
    [ JSON Extractor ]
    Names: productId
    JSON Path: $.products[*].id
    Match No: -1  // All matches

// Then use with ForEach Controller
[ ForEach Controller ]
Input variable prefix: productId
Output variable name: currentProductId
[ HTTP Request: Get Product Details ]
Path: /products/${currentProductId}
```

**3. Conditional Logic Optimization**

```
// BAD: Using JavaScript in If Controller (slow)
[ If Controller ]
Condition: "${userId}" != "" && "${userType}" == "premium"  // JavaScript evaluation

// GOOD: Use Variable Expression mode (fast)
[ If Controller ]
Condition: ${__jexl3("${userId}" != "" && "${userType}" == "premium")}
Interpret Condition as Variable Expression?: ✓

// BETTER: Pre-calculate condition in JSR223
[ JSR223 PreProcessor ]
Script: |
    boolean shouldExecute = vars.get("userId") != null && vars.get("userType") == "premium"
    vars.put("executePremiumFlow", shouldExecute.toString())

[ If Controller ]
Condition: ${executePremiumFlow}

// BEST: Use Switch Controller for multiple branches
[ Switch Controller ]
Switch Value: ${userType}
[ Simple Controller: guest ]
[ Simple Controller: standard ]
[ Simple Controller: premium ]
```

**4. Loop Optimization**

```
// BAD: Deeply nested loops
Thread Group: 100 users, 10 loops
└── [ Loop Controller: 5 times ]
    └── [ Loop Controller: 3 times ]
        └── HTTP Request
// Total iterations: 100 × 10 × 5 × 3 = 15,000

// GOOD: Calculate total and adjust
Thread Group: 100 users, 1 loop
└── [ Loop Controller: 150 times ]
    └── HTTP Request
// Same 15,000 iterations, less overhead

// BAD: Loop with many samplers
[ Loop Controller: 100 times ]
    HTTP Request: A
    HTTP Request: B
    HTTP Request: C
    HTTP Request: D
    HTTP Request: E
// 100 × 5 = 500 total requests

// GOOD: Parameterize and use single sampler
[ Loop Controller: 500 times ]
    [ JSR223 PreProcessor ]
    Script: vars.put("action", actions[count % actions.size()])
    HTTP Request: /api/${action}
// 500 requests, same coverage, less overhead
```

**5. JSR223 Script Optimization**

```groovy
// GOOD: Cache compiled scripts
[ JSR223 Sampler ]
Language: groovy
Cache compiled script if available: ✓

// GOOD: Use static variables for expensive objects
import groovy.json.JsonSlurper
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap

static def PARSER = new JsonSlurper()  // Created once per script
static def CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap()  // Shared across invocations

// GOOD: Avoid creating temporary objects in loops
// BAD:
1000.times {
    def json = new JsonSlurper().parseText(response)  // Created 1000 times
}

// GOOD:
def parser = new JsonSlurper()
1000.times {
    def json = parser.parseText(response)  // Reuse parser
}

// GOOD: Use primitive operations when possible
// BAD:
def sum = numbers.collect { it * 2 }.sum()

// GOOD:
def sum = 0
numbers.each { sum += it * 2 }

// GOOD: Cache repeated lookups
// BAD:
if (vars.get("userType") == "premium") { ... }
// later...
if (vars.get("userType") == "premium") { ... }

// GOOD:
def userType = vars.get("userType")
if (userType == "premium") { ... }
// later...
if (userType == "premium") { ... }
```

**6. Assertion Optimization**

```
// BAD: Multiple assertions on same response
[ HTTP Request ]
    [ Response Assertion: Status Code = 200 ]
    [ Response Assertion: Contains "success" ]
    [ Duration Assertion: < 2000 ]
    [ JSON Assertion: $.status == "OK" ]

// GOOD: Combine assertions in JSR223
[ JSR223 Assertion ]
Language: groovy
Script: |
    def failures = []

    if (prev.getResponseCode() != '200') {
        failures.add("Status code: ${prev.getResponseCode()}")
    }

    if (prev.getTime() > 2000) {
        failures.add("Response time: ${prev.getTime()}ms")
    }

    def json = new groovy.json.JsonSlurper().parseText(prev.getResponseDataAsString())
    if (json.status != 'OK') {
        failures.add("Status: ${json.status}")
    }

    if (failures) {
        AssertionResult.setFailure(true)
        AssertionResult.setFailureMessage(failures.join(', '))
    }

// GOOD: Sample assertions (1-5% of requests)
if (Math.random() < 0.05) {  // Only 5% of requests
    // Full validation
}
```

**7. Timer Optimization**

```
// BAD: Constant Timer between every request
[ Thread Group ]
    [ Constant Timer: 2000 ]
    HTTP Request: A
    [ Constant Timer: 2000 ]
    HTTP Request: B
    [ Constant Timer: 2000 ]
    HTTP Request: C

// GOOD: One timer at appropriate scope
[ Thread Group ]
    [ Simple Controller: User Journey ]
        [ Uniform Random Timer ]  ← Applies to all requests in controller
            Random Delay Maximum: 5000
            Constant Delay Offset: 1000
        HTTP Request: A
        HTTP Request: B
        HTTP Request: C

// GOOD: Use think times that match user behavior
// Reading content: Gaussian (human-like)
[ Gaussian Random Timer ]
    Deviation: 3000
    Constant Delay Offset: 5000  ← Average 5 seconds

// System processing: Constant
[ Constant Timer ]
    Thread Delay: 500  ← Fixed 500ms for system processing

// Random events: Uniform
[ Uniform Random Timer ]
    Random Delay Maximum: 10000
    Constant Delay Offset: 2000  ← 2-12 seconds
```

**8. Data-Driven Optimization**

```
// BAD: Large CSV with many columns
users.csv: id,username,password,email,firstName,lastName,address,city,state,zip,phone,role,department,managerId,hireDate,salary,status,lastLogin,preferences,...

// GOOD: Split into multiple CSVs
users_basic.csv: id,username,password,role
users_profile.csv: userId,firstName,lastName,email,phone
users_details.csv: userId,address,city,state,zip

// Load only what you need
[ CSV Data Set Config: Basic User Data ]
Filename: users_basic.csv
Variable Names: userId,username,password,role

[ CSV Data Set Config: Profile Data ]
Filename: users_profile.csv
Variable Names: userId,firstName,lastName,email,phone
Sharing mode: Current thread

// GOOD: Use __StringFromFile for simple cases
[ HTTP Request ]
Path: /users/${__StringFromFile(usernames.csv)}

// GOOD: Generate data on the fly
[ JSR223 PreProcessor ]
Script: |
