**Running Tests Faster with Concurrency:**

One of TestNG's most powerful features is its ability to run tests in parallel, dramatically reducing execution time for large test suites.

**Parallel Execution Levels:**

| Level | Description | Best For | Example |
|-------|-------------|----------|---------|
| **methods** | Run test methods in separate threads | Independent unit tests | `<suite parallel="methods" thread-count="10">` |
| **tests** | Run <test> tags in separate threads | Different test suites | `<suite parallel="tests" thread-count="5">` |
| **classes** | Run test classes in separate threads | Classes with independent tests | `<suite parallel="classes" thread-count="8">` |
| **instances** | Run instances in separate threads | Data-driven tests | `<suite parallel="instances" thread-count="4">` |

**Configuration Examples:**

```xml
<!-- Parallel by methods -->
<suite name="Method Parallel" parallel="methods" thread-count="10">
    <test name="Method Level Parallel">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.example.tests.ParallelTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>

<!-- Parallel by classes -->
<suite name="Class Parallel" parallel="classes" thread-count="5">
    <test name="Class Level Parallel">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.example.tests.TestClass1"/>
            <class name="com.example.tests.TestClass2"/>
            <class name="com.example.tests.TestClass3"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>

<!-- Parallel by tests -->
<suite name="Test Parallel" parallel="tests" thread-count="3">
    <test name="UI Tests" parallel="methods" thread-count="4">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.example.tests.UITest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
    <test name="API Tests" parallel="methods" thread-count="4">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.example.tests.APITest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
    <test name="Database Tests" parallel="methods" thread-count="2">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.example.tests.DatabaseTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>
```

