    public void testApiResponseWithSoftAssertions() {
        SoftAssert softAssert = new SoftAssert();

        // Simulate API response validation
        ApiResponse response = apiClient.getUserData(123);

        // Status code validation
        softAssert.assertEquals(response.getStatusCode(), 200, 
            "HTTP Status should be 200");

        // Response time validation
        softAssert.assertTrue(response.getResponseTime() < 500,
            String.format("Response time %d ms should be <500ms", 
                response.getResponseTime()));

        // Headers validation
        Map<String, String> headers = response.getHeaders();
        softAssert.assertNotNull(headers, "Headers should exist");

        if (headers != null) {
            softAssert.assertEquals(headers.get("Content-Type"), "application/json",
                "Content-Type header should be application/json");
            softAssert.assertNotNull(headers.get("X-Request-ID"),
                "X-Request-ID header should exist");
        }

        // Response body validation
        User user = response.getBody();
        softAssert.assertNotNull(user, "Response body should contain user");

        if (user != null) {
            softAssert.assertNotNull(user.getId(), "User ID should exist");
            softAssert.assertEquals(user.getEmail(), "expected@email.com",
                "Email should match expected");
            softAssert.assertTrue(user.isActive(), "User should be active");
        }

        // Report all failures at once
        softAssert.assertAll();
    }
}
```

**SoftAssert vs HardAssert Decision Guide:**

| Scenario | Use Hard Assert | Use Soft Assert |
|----------|-----------------|-----------------|
| Critical prerequisite (e.g., user exists) | ✅ Stop if missing | ❌ Risk of NPE |
| Validating multiple fields of an object | ❌ Stops after first failure | ✅ Get all failures |
| Performance validation | ✅ Stop if slow | ❌ Continue even if slow |
| API response validation | ❌ Want all validation results | ✅ Complete picture |
| Security checks | ✅ Stop immediately | ❌ Don't continue if insecure |
| UI element validation | ❌ Want all failures | ✅ Better for screenshots |
| Data-driven test with multiple rows | ✅ Each row independent | ❌ Mixing failures confusing |

**Best Practices for Soft Assertions:**

1. **Always call `assertAll()`** at the end of your test
2. **Use hard assertions for prerequisites** (objects that must exist)
3. **Provide meaningful messages** for each assertion
4. **Group related assertions** together logically
5. **Consider performance** - soft assertions still execute all code
6. **Don't overuse** - sometimes failing fast is better

```java
// Example: Mixed hard and soft assertions
@Test
public void mixedAssertionExample() {
    // Hard assertion for critical prerequisite
    Order order = orderService.getOrder(123);
    Assert.assertNotNull(order, "Order must exist");

    // Now use soft assertions for detailed validation
    SoftAssert softAssert = new SoftAssert();

    softAssert.assertEquals(order.getStatus(), OrderStatus.PAID,
        "Order should be paid");
    softAssert.assertEquals(order.getItems().size(), 3,
        "Order should have 3 items");
    softAssert.assertEquals(order.getTotal(), 299.97, 0.01,
        "Total should be correct");

    // Verify each item
    for (OrderItem item : order.getItems()) {
        softAssert.assertNotNull(item.getProductId(),
            "Each item should have product ID");
        softAssert.assertTrue(item.getQuantity() > 0,
            "Quantity should be positive");
    }

    softAssert.assertAll();
}
```

**The Art of Good Assertions:**

Well-written assertions are the difference between flaky, hard-to-debug tests and reliable, maintainable test suites.

**1. Provide Meaningful Messages**

Always include descriptive messages that help debug failures.

```java
// Bad
Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected);

// Good
Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected, 
    "User count should match after registration");

// Better - include context
Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected, 
    String.format("User count after adding %d users should be %d", 
        newUsers, expected));

// Best - include actual values for comparison
Assert.assertTrue(age >= 18,
    String.format("User age %d should be at least 18 for voting feature", age));
```

**2. One Logical Concept Per Test**

```java
// Bad - testing too many things
@Test
public void testUser() {
    User user = userService.createUser(...);
    Assert.assertNotNull(user);
    Assert.assertEquals(user.getEmail(), email);
    Assert.assertEquals(user.getName(), name);
    Assert.assertTrue(user.canLogin());
    Assert.assertTrue(user.canPurchase());
}

// Good - focused tests
@Test
public void testUserCreation_basicInfo() {
    User user = userService.createUser(email, name, age);
    Assert.assertNotNull(user, "User should be created");
    Assert.assertEquals(user.getEmail(), email, "Email should match");
    Assert.assertEquals(user.getName(), name, "Name should match");
}

@Test
public void testUserCreation_permissions() {
    User user = userService.createUser(...);
    Assert.assertTrue(user.canLogin(), "New user should be able to login");
    Assert.assertFalse(user.isAdmin(), "New user should not be admin");
}
```

**3. Test Both Positive and Negative Cases**

```java
@Test
public void testLogin_positive() {
    boolean result = authService.login(validUser, validPass);
    Assert.assertTrue(result, "Login should succeed with valid credentials");
}

@Test
public void testLogin_negative_wrongPassword() {
    boolean result = authService.login(validUser, wrongPass);
    Assert.assertFalse(result, "Login should fail with wrong password");
}

@Test
public void testLogin_negative_nullUser() {
    try {
        authService.login(null, "pass");
        Assert.fail("Should throw exception for null user");
    } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
        Assert.assertEquals(e.getMessage(), "User cannot be null");
    }
}
```

**4. Use Appropriate Assertion Methods**

```java
// Bad - using assertTrue for everything
Assert.assertTrue(value == 5);
Assert.assertTrue(object.equals(expected));
Assert.assertTrue(list.contains(item));

// Good - use specific assertions
Assert.assertEquals(value, 5);
Assert.assertEquals(object, expected);
Assert.assertTrue(list.contains(item), "List should contain item");

// Even better
Assert.assertEquals(value, 5, "Value should be 5");
Assert.assertEquals(object, expected, "Objects should be equal");
Assert.assertTrue(list.contains(item), 
    String.format("List %s should contain %s", list, item));
```

**5. Be Careful with Floating Point**

```java
// Bad - floating point precision issues
Assert.assertEquals(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3);  // May fail!

// Good - use delta
Assert.assertEquals(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3, 0.0001, 
    "Floating point calculation should be accurate within delta");

// Better - use BigDecimal for precise calculations
BigDecimal result = new BigDecimal("0.1").add(new BigDecimal("0.2"));
Assert.assertEquals(result, new BigDecimal("0.3"));
```

**6. Assert Collections Properly**

```java
@Test
public void testCollections() {
    List<String> actual = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
    List<String> expected = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");

    // Check entire list equality
    Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected, "Lists should be equal");

    // Check size
    Assert.assertEquals(actual.size(), 3, "List should have 3 elements");

    // Check contains
    Assert.assertTrue(actual.contains("b"), "List should contain 'b'");

    // Check order matters
    List<String> unsorted = Arrays.asList("c", "a", "b");
    Assert.assertNotEquals(actual, unsorted, "Order matters");

    // Check content ignoring order
    Set<String> actualSet = new HashSet<>(actual);
    Set<String> expectedSet = new HashSet<>(expected);
    Assert.assertEquals(actualSet, expectedSet, "Sets should be equal");
}
```

**7. Use Custom Assertion Methods for Complex Objects**

```java
public class UserAssertions {

    public static void assertUserValid(User user, String expectedEmail, String expectedName) {
        Assert.assertNotNull(user, "User should not be null");
        Assert.assertNotNull(user.getId(), "User ID should be generated");
        Assert.assertEquals(user.getEmail(), expectedEmail, "Email should match");
        Assert.assertEquals(user.getName(), expectedName, "Name should match");
        Assert.assertTrue(user.isActive(), "User should be active");
        Assert.assertNotNull(user.getCreatedAt(), "Creation date should be set");
    }

    public static void assertOrderValid(Order order, String userId, int expectedItemCount) {
        Assert.assertNotNull(order, "Order should not be null");
        Assert.assertEquals(order.getUserId(), userId, "User ID should match");
        Assert.assertEquals(order.getItems().size(), expectedItemCount, 
            "Item count should match");
        Assert.assertNotNull(order.getOrderDate(), "Order date should be set");
        Assert.assertEquals(order.getStatus(), OrderStatus.PENDING, 
            "New orders should be pending");
    }
}

// Usage in tests
@Test
public void testCreateUser() {
    User user = userService.createUser("test@test.com", "Test User");
    UserAssertions.assertUserValid(user, "test@test.com", "Test User");
}

@Test
public void testCreateOrder() {
    Order order = orderService.createOrder(userId, items);
    UserAssertions.assertOrderValid(order, userId, 3);
}
```

**8. Don't Assert Implementation Details**

```java
// Bad - asserting implementation details
@Test
public void testCacheImplementation() {
    Cache cache = new Cache();
    cache.put("key", "value");

    // Asserting internal structure - fragile!
    Assert.assertTrue(cache.getInternalMap().containsKey("key"));
}

// Good - assert behavior
@Test
public void testCacheBehavior() {
    Cache cache = new Cache();
    cache.put("key", "value");

    String result = cache.get("key");
    Assert.assertEquals(result, "value", "Should retrieve stored value");

    // Test expiration
    Thread.sleep(6000); // Wait for expiration
    String expired = cache.get("key");
    Assert.assertNull(expired, "Value should expire after 5 seconds");
}
```

**9. Handle Timeouts and Performance**

```java
@Test(timeOut = 1000)
public void testPerformance() {
    long result = expensiveOperation();
    Assert.assertEquals(result, expected);
}

// Alternative: measure and assert
@Test
public void testPerformanceWithAssertion() {
    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

    long result = expensiveOperation();

    long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
    Assert.assertTrue(duration < 500,
        String.format("Operation took %d ms, expected <500 ms", duration));

    Assert.assertEquals(result, expected, "Result should be correct");
}
```

**10. Use ExpectedException for Exception Testing**

```java
// Method 1: expectedExceptions attribute
@Test(expectedExceptions = IllegalArgumentException.class,
      expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp = ".*negative.*")
public void testWithdrawNegativeAmount() {
    account.withdraw(-100);
}

// Method 2: try-catch with assertions
@Test
public void testWithdrawWithInsufficientFunds() {
    account.setBalance(50);

    try {
        account.withdraw(100);
        Assert.fail("Should throw exception for insufficient funds");
    } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
        Assert.assertEquals(e.getCurrentBalance(), 50);
        Assert.assertEquals(e.getRequestedAmount(), 100);
        Assert.assertTrue(e.getMessage().contains("Insufficient funds"));
    }
}
```

**11. Assertions in Data-Driven Tests**

```java
@DataProvider(name = "testData")
public Object[][] getTestData() {
    return new Object[][]{
        {new User("valid@test.com", 30), true},
        {new User("invalid", 30), false},
        {new User("test@test.com", 15), false},
        {null, false}
    };
}

@Test(dataProvider = "testData")
public void testUserValidation(User user, boolean expectedValid) {
    try {
        boolean isValid = userService.validateUser(user);
        Assert.assertEquals(isValid, expectedValid,
            String.format("User %s validation should be %s", user, expectedValid));
    } catch (NullPointerException e) {
        if (expectedValid) {
            Assert.fail("Unexpected NPE for user: " + user);
        }
        // Expected exception, test passes
    }
}
```

**12. Assertion Checklist**

Use this checklist when writing assertions:

- [ ] Does the assertion have a meaningful message?
- [ ] Does the assertion validate the behavior, not implementation?
- [ ] Is this the right assertion method (equality, true/false, null)?
- [ ] Have I considered both positive and negative cases?
- [ ] Is the test name descriptive enough?
- [ ] Does the assertion help debug when it fails?
- [ ] Have I handled floating point precision correctly?
- [ ] For collections, have I considered order and duplicates?
- [ ] Are my assertions independent of each other?
- [ ] Would someone understand why this test failed from the message?

**13. Common Assertion Anti-Patterns**

| Anti-Pattern | Example | Better Approach |
|--------------|---------|-----------------|
| **Asserting without message** | `Assert.assertTrue(result);` | `Assert.assertTrue(result, "User should be logged in");` |
| **Asserting too much** | One test with 20 assertions | Split into multiple focused tests |
| **Ignoring order in collections** | Assuming list order | Use sets if order doesn't matter |
| **Floating point without delta** | `Assert.assertEquals(0.1+0.2, 0.3);` | `Assert.assertEquals(..., 0.0001);` |
| **Empty catch blocks** | `catch (Exception e) {}` | Use `expectedExceptions` or rethrow |
| **Sleeps instead of assertions** | `Thread.sleep(5000);` | Use proper waits/timeouts |

**14. Summary: Assertion Best Practices**

```java
@Test(description = "Complete example of good assertion practices")
public void testGoodAssertions() {
    // 1. Check prerequisites with hard assertions
    User user = userService.findUser(123);
    Assert.assertNotNull(user, "Test user must exist");

    // 2. Use soft assertions for multiple validations
    SoftAssert softAssert = new SoftAssert();

    // 3. Provide context in messages
    softAssert.assertEquals(user.getName(), "John Doe",
        String.format("User %d name should be John Doe", user.getId()));

    // 4. Use appropriate assertion methods
    softAssert.assertTrue(user.isActive(), "User should be active");
    softAssert.assertFalse(user.isBlocked(), "User should not be blocked");

    // 5. Validate collections properly
    List<Order> orders = user.getRecentOrders();
    softAssert.assertNotNull(orders, "Orders list should not be null");
    softAssert.assertTrue(orders.size() <= 10, "Should have at most 10 recent orders");

    // 6. Check business rules
    if (user.getAge() < 18) {
        softAssert.assertFalse(user.canPurchase(),
            "Underage users should not be able to purchase");
    }

    // 7. Report all failures
    softAssert.assertAll();
}
```

**Key Takeaway:**

Good assertions are the difference between flaky, hard-to-debug tests and reliable, maintainable test suites. Invest time in writing clear, focused assertions with meaningful messages that will help you quickly identify and fix issues when they arise.

**Skipping Tests Intelligently:**

TestNG provides several ways to skip tests based on conditions, both in code and in testng.xml.

**1. Basic Enable/Disable in XML:**

```xml
<!-- Disable entire test -->
<test name="Disabled Test" enabled="false">
    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.DisabledTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>

<!-- Disable specific methods -->
<test name="Partial Disable">
    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.MyTest">
            <methods>
                <include name=".*"/>
                <exclude name="testNotReady"/>
                <exclude name="testUnderConstruction"/>
                <exclude name=".*Broken.*"/>
            </methods>
        </class>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**2. Conditional Skipping with Groups:**

```xml
<test name="Conditional Groups">
    <!-- Include based on environment parameter -->
    <groups>
        <run>
            <include name="${environment}"/>
        </run>
    </groups>

    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.EnvironmentSpecificTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**3. Using BeanShell for Complex Conditions:**

```xml
<test name="BeanShell Test">
    <method-selectors>
        <method-selector>
            <script language="beanshell">
                <![CDATA[
                    // Skip if running in production and test is destructive
                    if (System.getProperty("environment").equals("production")) {
                        // Check if test method has destructive annotation
                        return !testngMethod.getMethodName().contains("Delete");
                    }
                    return true;
                ]]>
            </script>
        </method-selector>
    </method-selectors>

    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.ProductionSafeTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**4. Conditional Skipping with System Properties:**

```xml
<test name="System Property Test">
    <method-selectors>
        <method-selector>
            <script language="beanshell">
                <![CDATA[
                    String browser = System.getProperty("browser", "chrome");
                    // Skip Safari tests if browser is not safari
                    if (testngMethod.getMethodName().contains("Safari")) {
                        return browser.equals("safari");
                    }
                    return true;
                ]]>
            </script>
        </method-selector>
    </method-selectors>

    <classes>
        <class name="com.example.BrowserSpecificTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>
```

**5. Environment-Aware Test Selection:**

```xml
<suite name="Environment-Aware Suite">
    <parameter name="environment" value="${env}"/>

    <test name="Environment Tests">
        <method-selectors>
            <method-selector>
                <script language="beanshell">
                    <![CDATA[
                        String env = System.getProperty("environment", "dev");

                        // Get test annotations
                        org.testng.annotations.ITestAnnotation test = 
                            testngMethod.getConstructorOrMethod().getMethod()
                                .getAnnotation(org.testng.annotations.Test.class);

                        if (test != null) {
                            String[] groups = test.groups();
                            for (String group : groups) {
                                // Skip tests marked as "prod-only" in non-prod
                                if (group.equals("prod-only") && !env.equals("production")) {
                                    return false;
                                }
                                // Skip tests marked as "dev-only" in prod
                                if (group.equals("dev-only") && env.equals("production")) {
                                    return false;
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        return true;
                    ]]>
                </script>
            </method-selector>
        </method-selectors>

        <packages>
            <package name="com.example.tests.*"/>
        </packages>
    </test>
</suite>
```

**6. Skipping Tests with Annotations:**

```java
public class ConditionalSkipTest {

    @Test(enabled = false)  // Simple disable
    public void testNotReady() { }

    @Test
    @SkipIf(condition = "${environment == 'production'}")
    public void testSkipInProduction() { }

    @Test
    @SkipIf(condition = "${browser == 'safari'}")
    public void testSkipInSafari() { }
}

// Custom annotation
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target({ElementType.METHOD, ElementType.TYPE})
public @interface SkipIf {
    String condition();
}

// Listener to process custom annotation
public class SkipIfListener implements IMethodInterceptor {
    @Override
    public List<IMethodInstance> intercept(List<IMethodInstance> methods, 
                                            ITestContext context) {
        List<IMethodInstance> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (IMethodInstance method : methods) {
            SkipIf skipIf = method.getMethod().getConstructorOrMethod().getMethod()
                .getAnnotation(SkipIf.class);

            if (skipIf != null) {
                String condition = skipIf.condition();
                // Evaluate condition using expression language
                if (evaluateCondition(condition)) {
                    System.out.println("Skipping " + method.getMethod().getMethodName() + 
                        " because condition: " + condition);
                    continue;  // Skip this method
                }
            }
            result.add(method);
        }
        return result;
    }

    private boolean evaluateCondition(String condition) {
        // Implementation to evaluate condition
        return false;
    }
}
```

**7. Runtime Skipping with SkipException:**

```java
public class RuntimeSkipTest {

    @Test
    public void testConditionalSkip() {
        String environment = System.getProperty("environment");

        if ("production".equals(environment)) {
            throw new SkipException("Skipping this test in production");
        }

        // Test logic
    }

