    Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
    int sum = calculator.add(5, 3);
    assertEquals(8, sum, "5 + 3 should equal 8");
}
```

**9. Don't Test Implementation Details**

```java
// BAD - Testing private method through reflection or internal state
@Test
void testPrivateMethod() throws Exception {
    Method method = MyClass.class.getDeclaredMethod("privateMethod");
    method.setAccessible(true);
    Object result = method.invoke(new MyClass());
    assertEquals("expected", result);
}

// GOOD - Test public behavior
@Test
void testPublicBehavior() {
    MyClass obj = new MyClass();
    String result = obj.publicMethodThatUsesPrivateMethod();
    assertEquals("expected", result);
}
```

**10. Follow the F.I.R.S.T. Principles**

| Principle | Meaning | How to Achieve |
|-----------|---------|----------------|
| **Fast** | Tests run quickly | Mock external dependencies, avoid I/O |
| **Independent** | Tests don't depend on each other | Fresh setup for each test |
| **Repeatable** | Same result every time | No random data, controlled environment |
| **Self-Validating** | Tests pass/fail clearly | Use assertions, no manual checking |
| **Timely** | Written at the right time | Write tests before or with code |

**Test Organization Checklist:**

- [ ] Test class name corresponds to production class
- [ ] Test methods are independent and can run in any order
- [ ] Setup and teardown methods are used appropriately
- [ ] Tests are grouped logically (using @Nested if needed)
- [ ] Test names are descriptive and follow a consistent pattern
- [ ] Each test verifies one specific behavior
- [ ] Edge cases and error conditions are tested
- [ ] Tests are fast and don't depend on external systems
- [ ] Assertions are specific and include meaningful messages
- [ ] Code coverage is adequate (but not the only metric)

**Example: Well-Organized Test Class**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

@DisplayName("Order Service Tests")
@Tag("unit")
class OrderServiceTest {

    private OrderService orderService;
    private PaymentService paymentService;
    private InventoryService inventoryService;
    private Order testOrder;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        paymentService = mock(PaymentService.class);
        inventoryService = mock(InventoryService.class);
        orderService = new OrderService(paymentService, inventoryService);

        testOrder = new Order("user123");
        testOrder.addItem(new Item("product1", 2, 10.0));
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("Order Creation")
    class CreateOrderTests {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should create order with valid items")
        void createOrder_withValidItems_shouldSucceed() {
            when(inventoryService.checkAvailability("product1", 2)).thenReturn(true);

            Order created = orderService.createOrder(testOrder);

            assertNotNull(created);
            assertNotNull(created.getId());
            assertEquals(OrderStatus.PENDING, created.getStatus());
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should throw exception when items out of stock")
        void createOrder_withOutOfStock_shouldThrow() {
            when(inventoryService.checkAvailability("product1", 2)).thenReturn(false);

            assertThrows(OutOfStockException.class, 
                () -> orderService.createOrder(testOrder));
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should throw exception when order has no items")
        void createOrder_withEmptyOrder_shouldThrow() {
            Order emptyOrder = new Order("user123");

            assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
                () -> orderService.createOrder(emptyOrder));
        }
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("Order Payment")
    class PaymentTests {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should process payment successfully")
        void processPayment_withValidCard_shouldSucceed() {
            when(paymentService.charge(anyDouble(), anyString())).thenReturn(true);

            boolean paid = orderService.processPayment(testOrder, "valid-card");

            assertTrue(paid);
            assertEquals(OrderStatus.PAID, testOrder.getStatus());
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should handle payment failure")
        void processPayment_withInvalidCard_shouldFail() {
            when(paymentService.charge(anyDouble(), anyString())).thenReturn(false);

            boolean paid = orderService.processPayment(testOrder, "invalid-card");

            assertFalse(paid);
            assertEquals(OrderStatus.PENDING, testOrder.getStatus());
        }
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("Order Cancellation")
    class CancellationTests {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should cancel pending order")
        void cancelOrder_withPendingStatus_shouldSucceed() {
            boolean cancelled = orderService.cancelOrder(testOrder);

            assertTrue(cancelled);
            assertEquals(OrderStatus.CANCELLED, testOrder.getStatus());
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should not cancel already shipped order")
        void cancelOrder_withShippedStatus_shouldFail() {
            testOrder.setStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);

            boolean cancelled = orderService.cancelOrder(testOrder);

            assertFalse(cancelled);
            assertEquals(OrderStatus.SHIPPED, testOrder.getStatus());
        }
    }
}
```

**Summary: Keys to Great Test Classes**

1. **One test class per production class** - Clear mapping
2. **Use @Nested for logical grouping** - Better organization
3. **Descriptive test names** - Self-documenting
4. **Independent tests** - No shared mutable state
5. **Fast execution** - Mock external dependencies
6. **Comprehensive coverage** - Happy paths and edge cases
7. **Readable assertions** - Clear verification
8. **Proper setup/teardown** - Clean test fixtures
9. **Follow naming conventions** - Consistency across team
10. **Regular refactoring** - Keep tests maintainable

**Assertions: The Heart of Test Automation**

Assertions are statements that verify whether the actual outcome of a test matches the expected outcome. Without assertions, tests can run but provide no validation—they're just executing code without checking results.

**Why Assertions Matter:**

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Validation** | Confirm that the system behaves as expected |
| **Documentation** | Assertions document what the test verifies |
| **Failure Detection** | Automatically detect when something goes wrong |
| **Regression Prevention** | Catch unintended changes in behavior |
| **Quality Gates** | Provide pass/fail criteria for CI/CD pipelines |

**The JUnit Assertions Class:**

JUnit 5 provides the `org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions` class with static methods for all common assertion types. Simply import it statically and use the methods directly.

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class AssertionExample {

    @Test
    void testSomething() {
        int actual = 5;
        int expected = 5;

        // Basic assertion
        assertEquals(expected, actual, "Values should be equal");
    }
}
```

**Assertion Method Categories:**

| Category | Purpose | Example Methods |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| **Equality** | Compare values for equality | `assertEquals`, `assertNotEquals` |
| **Boolean** | Verify true/false conditions | `assertTrue`, `assertFalse` |
| **Null Checks** | Verify null/non-null | `assertNull`, `assertNotNull` |
| **Object Identity** | Check object references | `assertSame`, `assertNotSame` |
| **Arrays/Collections** | Compare arrays and collections | `assertArrayEquals`, `assertIterableEquals` |
| **Exceptions** | Verify exception throwing | `assertThrows`, `assertDoesNotThrow` |
| **Timeouts** | Verify execution time | `assertTimeout`, `assertTimeoutPreemptively` |
| **Grouped** | Multiple assertions together | `assertAll` |
| **Fail** | Force test failure | `fail` |

**Assertion Lifecycle in a Test:**

```java
@Test
void testUserCreation() {
    // Arrange
    UserService userService = new UserService();
    User newUser = new User("john@example.com", "John Doe");

    // Act
    User createdUser = userService.createUser(newUser);

    // Assert - Multiple assertions verify different aspects
    assertNotNull(createdUser, "User should not be null");
    assertNotNull(createdUser.getId(), "User ID should be generated");
    assertEquals("john@example.com", createdUser.getEmail(), "Email should match");
    assertEquals("John Doe", createdUser.getName(), "Name should match");
    assertTrue(createdUser.isActive(), "User should be active by default");
}
```

**What Happens When Assertions Fail:**

When an assertion fails, JUnit throws an `AssertionFailedError`. This:
1. Immediately stops the current test method
2. Marks the test as FAILED in the report
3. Records the assertion error message and stack trace
4. Continues with the next test method

```java
@Test
void testThatWillFail() {
    int actual = 5;
    int expected = 10;

    // This will throw AssertionFailedError with the message
    assertEquals(expected, actual, 
        "Expected 10 but got 5");

    // This line will never execute
    System.out.println("This won't print");
}
```

**Assertion Messages:**

Always include descriptive messages to help debug failures.

```java
// Bad - no context when it fails
assertTrue(user.isActive());

// Good - clear what failed
assertTrue(user.isActive(), 
    "User should be active after registration");

// Even better - include actual values
assertTrue(user.isActive(), 
    () -> String.format("User %s should be active but was %s", 
        user.getEmail(), user.getStatus()));

// Lazy evaluation - message only constructed if assertion fails
assertTrue(user.isActive(), 
    () -> expensiveMessageConstruction());
```

